// =============================================================================
// core/id/mod.vr — unique identifiers
// =============================================================================
// Layer: 2 (needs a clock and entropy)
// =============================================================================
//

// Generators for identifiers that must not collide. They were in
// `core/base/` — where `core.id.snowflake` claimed that a Snowflake ID
// is a primitive of the LANGUAGE, alongside Maybe and Result. It is not:
// these read a wall clock and draw randomness, which is why the ring law
// measured `base(r0) -> time.system_time(r2)` and pointed here.
//

//  * `uuid`      — RFC 9562 UUIDs.
//  * `ulid`      — lexicographically sortable, timestamp-prefixed.
//  * `nanoid`    — short, URL-safe, entropy-only.
//  * `snowflake` — 64-bit, timestamp + machine + sequence.
//

// All four draw from `core.random.secure`; none is a hash, and none is
// a secret. An identifier being unpredictable is a property of the
// generator, not of the format — `ulid` and `snowflake` embed a
// timestamp by design and reveal creation time to anyone holding one.
//

// =============================================================================

module core.id;

public module uuid;
public module ulid;
public module nanoid;
public module snowflake;
